<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>Corsac</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>corc</AssemblyName>
    <!-- The programs this one replaced, still reachable by their old names. -->
    <ToolAliases>corlink=link;build=build</ToolAliases>
    <StartupObject>Corsac.Program</StartupObject>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>

    <!--
      HOW THE COMPILER COLLECTS ITS GARBAGE, which is worth saying out loud
      because it is worth about a third of the wall clock.

      The compiler allocates hard and keeps almost nothing: the kernel's 118
      units churn 24 GiB and hold under 200 MB. With the default workstation
      collector, eight worker threads doing that spend their time stopped for
      each other. The same work cost 2.2 times the processor time at eight
      workers as at one, and eight finished no sooner than four.

      So: the server collector, which gives each heap its own thread, with
      the background collector off (there is nothing long-lived for it to
      find) and four heaps rather than one per core, which was pure waste on
      a large machine. And a hard limit, which is the part that matters for
      memory: without it the collector grows to 845 MB because nothing tells
      it not to, and with it the same compile peaks well below that, because
      a limit makes it collect instead of grow.

      768 MB, and not less, because the limit is a WALL and not a target: the
      biggest single compile this repository does, the whole class library
      inline as a fifteen megabyte object, already holds about 270 MB by
      itself, and a cap of 256 MB left it nothing. It survived alone and
      aborted inside the ELF writer when the test suite ran several
      compilers at once, which is the worst way to find out. Three times the
      largest known live set is the headroom; the sweep says the roomier cap
      is FASTER anyway (6.6 s against 7.3 s over the kernel), because the
      collector that is not fighting a wall runs less often.

      Kernel, eight workers: 12.0 s and 371 MB becomes about 6.6 s.
      Never slower than the default at any worker count. Every one of these
      can be overridden by its DOTNET_ environment variable for a machine
      that wants something else; a build that needs a bigger live heap than
      this should raise DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit rather than remove it.
    -->
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>false</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
    <GCHeapCount>4</GCHeapCount>
    <GCHeapHardLimit>805306368</GCHeapHardLimit>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The same two numbers again, spelled the way the .NET SDK wants them. The
    properties above are what THIS repository's build tool reads; the SDK
    knows nothing of them and writes a runtimeconfig.json with the collector
    but not the cap, so a compiler built with `dotnet build` ran with no
    limit at all and nobody could tell from the outside. Both spellings are
    kept so the two builds cannot drift apart again.
  -->
  <!--
    Guarded so that only the SDK sees them: this repository's toolchain
    rebuilds itself with the compiler it already has, and that compiler
    evaluates this file BEFORE the new one exists. An item type the old
    evaluator has never heard of would stop the bootstrap; a condition it
    finds false is skipped before the type is looked at.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildRuntimeType)' != ''">
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.GC.HeapCount" Value="$(GCHeapCount)" />
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.GC.HeapHardLimit" Value="$(GCHeapHardLimit)" />
  </ItemGroup>



  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="src/**/*.cs" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../linker/linker.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../tools/build/build.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
